No. The available evidence does not support tier-1 certification.

The 15% transient headroom requirement is not a footnote. Disk-pressure alerting provides detection; it neither reserves the required capacity nor prevents compaction failure. Certification remains blocked until the assessment establishes:

- a production capacity policy reserving at least the tested headroom, with additional margin for workload variation;
- behavior when available space falls below 15%, including data integrity and service availability;
- alert thresholds and automated or documented remediation before exhaustion;
- peak-space duration and frequency;
- repeatability across representative datasets, utilization levels, and concurrent compactions;
- the organization’s tier-1 availability and recovery criteria.

Use this in the assessment:

> **Tier-1 readiness: not yet validated.** KiteDB reduced write amplification from 14.8 to 7.1 in one 4-hour benchmark, but major compactions required up to 15% transient disk headroom. Existing disk-pressure alerting detects capacity risk but does not guarantee the required capacity. Tier-1 certification is pending capacity controls and disk-pressure failure testing.

My recommendation is to mark certification as pending, not approved, until those controls and tests are documented.

The new information addresses the **capacity-policy blocker**, provided the 25% headroom policy is enforced and measured on the same basis as the benchmark’s 15% requirement. It does not, by itself, validate the full tier-1 certification; the benchmark remains one 4-hour run.

Use:

> **Transient-capacity requirement: controlled.** Major compactions required up to 15% transient disk headroom in the tested workload. Production policy requires 25% headroom on every host, providing a 10-percentage-point margin against the observed peak. Compliance is monitored through [control/telemetry]. This conclusion is limited to the tested workload; peak utilization will be validated under representative production compactions.

Before treating this item as unblocked, attach evidence that:

1. the 25% threshold is mandatory and continuously enforced, not only alerted;
2. both percentages use the same denominator and measurement method;
3. compaction cannot begin with less than the required margin, or another control prevents exhaustion;
4. current production hosts comply with the policy.

My recommendation: mark the **15% capacity item resolved by an existing control** if those four points are documented, but do not certify overall tier-1 readiness solely from this result.
